Equal-valued actual exchange: indices 0,1; before-values 2,2; root parking true.
Known snapshot cap8 rejects true; reserved9 plus first online event rejects true; input cap1 rejects true; exact10 retains 10 units.
\image(figures/heapsort_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Two-value cases need nine snapshot units plus one swap event; cap 9 admits snapshots but rejects before the exchange. Empty input still has two empty checkpoint records.)
\alttext(Paired bars show known snapshot units growing from 2 to 4 to 9, and one additional online swap unit in each two-value case.)

Equal keys need care when discussing stability. The integer API has no identity
labels, but the two-value trace above records an actual exchange of positions 0
and 1 when both contain 2. If those equal keys carried original labels a,b, that
exchange would reverse their order. This provides a concrete instability
witness; strict candidate comparisons do not make the whole sorting algorithm
stable.

Owned output, snapshots and event scalars retain no input references and copy
independently. Legacy in-place calls intentionally mutate their vector; owned
run/trace calls never do. Independent calls share no mutable state. Input caps
are ignored by the legacy methods, and capture caps are ignored without tracing.
Numerical timing, cache superiority and universal comparisons with quicksort or
mergesort are outside this evidence.

> What the examples establish

The original six values show the two invariants that drive heapsort: heap
dominance exposes the next maximum, and every root parking extends a sorted
suffix. The exact height sum proves linear construction, while actual comparison
curves distinguish a worst-case upper bound from easy inputs. Constant auxiliary
storage belongs to the in-place algorithm; retaining full histories has an
explicit, much larger cost. No external dataset is needed to test this finite
ordering problem.

Primary sources are J. W. J. Williams (1964), [Algorithm 232:
Heapsort](https://doi.org/10.1145/512274.512284), and R. W. Floyd (1964),
[Algorithm 245: Treesort 3](https://doi.org/10.1145/355588.365103). The library
uses bottom-up construction followed by its original strict left-before-right
swap-based repairs; it does not claim to reproduce every historical procedure or
comparison count.

Verification compares legacy built heaps and sorted outputs with a frozen
implementation, checks every ternary input through eight positions against
independent heap/sorted-suffix/multiset certificates, and checks actual
swap-prefix states and selected work under sanitizers. Extremes, ownership,
equal swaps, disabled/exact/one-short known and online capture caps accompany
the generated families. API examples, complete programs, inspected figures and
both exports are verified before retiring the original report.
